A rollback plan defines how an organization can return to the original environment if a migrated workload does not operate correctly in Azure.

A rollback strategy may include:

* Maintaining the original environment temporarily
* Preserving synchronized data
* Documenting restoration procedures
* Defining rollback decision criteria
* Maintaining DNS or traffic-routing options
* Establishing communication procedures

For example, if a migrated application experiences unexpected performance problems after production cutover, the organization may temporarily redirect users back to the original environment while the Azure deployment is investigated.

A real-life use case is a business-critical application where migration failure cannot be allowed to result in prolonged downtime.

Rollback planning provides an additional layer of protection during high-risk migrations.
